    "title": "GST for Freelancers and Consultants in India: A Practical Business Checklist",
    "excerpt": "Understand the GST questions freelancers should review before invoicing clients, including registration, place of supply, records and return preparation.",
    "metaTitle": "GST for Freelancers & Consultants in India | FileFast Global",
    "metaDescription": "A practical GST checklist for Indian freelancers and consultants covering registration review, invoices, client location, records and return readiness.",
    "content": "Freelancers, consultants and independent professionals may provide services to Indian or overseas clients. GST treatment depends on the nature of the service, turnover, location of supplier and recipient, place-of-supply rules and any applicable exemption or special provision. Do not assume that every freelancer has the same GST obligation.\n\n## 1. Map your work and clients\nCreate a simple service register showing:\n- What service you provide and how it is described in your contract.\n- Whether the customer is a business or an individual.\n- Customer location and billing address.\n- Whether the customer is in India or outside India.\n- Invoice currency, payment method and date of receipt.\n- Whether the work is recurring, milestone-based or one-time.\n\n## 2. Review registration applicability\nCheck the current registration provisions against your aggregate turnover, state, service type and business model. Certain activities and transaction patterns can have special rules. A threshold should not be applied without checking the full facts and current notifications.\n\n## 3. Prepare clear invoices\nWhere GST registration and tax invoice requirements apply, keep invoice details consistent with the contract and books. Maintain invoice number and date, supplier and recipient details, service description, taxable value, tax treatment and payment status. Do not charge GST merely because a client asks for it if you are not legally entitled to collect it.\n\n## 4. Overseas clients need a separate review\nA payment from a foreign client does not automatically make a transaction an export of services. Review the statutory conditions, place of supply, recipient status, intermediary-related concerns where relevant, foreign exchange realisation and documentation. If the supply qualifies as export, check the applicable LUT/bond and reporting process for the relevant financial year.\n\n## 5. Keep records ready\n- Signed proposal, engagement letter or contract.\n- Invoices and credit notes.\n- Bank advice and inward remittance evidence, where applicable.\n- Platform statements and payment gateway fees.\n- Expense invoices and ITC support.\n- GST returns and reconciliations, if registered.\n\n## 6. Monthly review routine\nAt each month-end, reconcile invoices raised, advances received, credit notes, bank receipts and platform deductions. Review return data before filing and keep a copy of the filed return and working papers.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Describing a service vaguely on the invoice.\n- Treating all foreign receipts as exports without checking conditions.\n- Ignoring platform commissions and payment gateway deductions.\n- Claiming ITC without checking eligibility and restrictions.\n- Mixing personal and business transactions without a clear audit trail.\n\n## How FileFast can help\nFileFast Global supports GST registration reviews, return preparation, invoice-data checks and export-documentation workflows. The final tax treatment depends on your facts and the law applicable to the relevant period.\n\nOfficial reference: https://www.gst.gov.in/"
