    "slug": "export-services-lut-documentation-checklist",
    "category": "Export Compliance",
    "title": "Export of Services Under GST: LUT and Documentation Checklist for Indian Businesses",
    "excerpt": "Organise contracts, invoices, foreign remittance evidence and return records when reviewing whether an overseas service transaction qualifies as export.",
    "metaTitle": "Export of Services Under GST: LUT Checklist | FileFast",
    "metaDescription": "A practical export-of-services GST checklist covering eligibility review, LUT, invoices, remittance evidence, records and return reconciliation.",
    "content": "Indian service providers working with overseas customers should review the GST treatment of each transaction before applying export terminology or issuing an invoice without tax. A foreign customer or foreign-currency payment alone does not establish that a supply qualifies as export of services.\n\n## 1. Review the statutory conditions\nCheck each condition in the definition of export of services under the IGST Act, including supplier location, recipient location, place of supply, permitted payment realisation and whether supplier and recipient are merely establishments of the same person. The actual contract and service delivery model matter.\n\n## 2. Review place of supply\nThe general place-of-supply rule may not apply to every service. Specific categories and intermediary-related facts can change the analysis. Document the service, recipient, contractual role and who benefits from the supply. Obtain specialist advice where the arrangement involves agents, platforms or multiple establishments.\n\n## 3. LUT readiness\nWhere the supply qualifies and the business intends to export without payment of integrated tax under LUT, check the current GST portal procedure and eligibility for the relevant financial year. Retain the filed LUT acknowledgement and ensure invoice and return records are consistent.\n\n## 4. Prepare the document file\n- Signed contract, statement of work or engagement letter.\n- Customer legal name, address and overseas location evidence.\n- Detailed service description and deliverables.\n- Export invoice and invoice register.\n- LUT acknowledgement, where applicable.\n- Bank advice, inward remittance certificate or other permitted realisation evidence, as relevant.\n- Email approvals, project milestones and delivery records.\n- GST returns and reconciliation workpapers.\n\n## 5. Reconcile invoices and receipts\nMap each invoice to the contract, service period, receipt and bank credit. Explain partial payments, advances, credit notes, refunds and exchange-rate differences. Retain an open-items list for invoices not yet realised or requiring follow-up.\n\n## 6. Review related expenses and ITC separately\nInput tax credit on freight, software, professional services and other expenses must be evaluated under the applicable eligibility rules and restrictions. The fact that the business exports services does not make every expense creditable.\n\n## How FileFast can help\nFileFast Global can assist with export-document organisation, LUT workflows, invoice checks and GST return reconciliation. Complex place-of-supply, intermediary or cross-border legal questions may require specialist tax advice.\n\nOfficial reference: https://cbic-gst.gov.in/"
